{
  "_comment": [
    "One corpus, three implementations. A message body is turned into readable text",
    "in three places -- backend/utils/rich_text.py for email and notification",
    "previews, frontend/src/utils/richText.js for the web app (via the DOM), and",
    "frontend-v2/src/utils/richText.ts for mobile (via regex, because native has no",
    "DOM). All three docstrings claim to mirror each other. Two of them did not.",
    "",
    "On 2026-09-03 the mobile one was found leaving uncommon named entities",
    "undecoded, so an announcement containing 'caf&eacute;' or '&copy;' read",
    "correctly in email and on the web and showed the raw entity on a phone; and it",
    "decoded &nbsp; to a plain space where the other two produce U+00A0.",
    "",
    "Expected values are generated from backend/utils/rich_text.py, which is",
    "canonical: it is the one that decides what gets STORED and what an email says.",
    "Each side has a test that reads this file. Adding a case here makes all three",
    "prove they still agree.",
    "",
    "Not included: '&notarealentity;'. Python's html.unescape and the browser DOM",
    "both implement HTML5's legacy no-semicolon entities and read that as '&not' +",
    "'arealentity;'. A regex decoder reasonably does not, and it is not a contract",
    "worth forcing on three implementations for input nobody types."
  ],
  "cases": [
    {
      "why": "plain text passes through untouched",
      "input": "Early dismissal at noon",
      "text": "Early dismissal at noon",
      "isHtml": false,
      "isBlank": false
    },
    {
      "why": "a paragraph is just its text",
      "input": "<p>Early dismissal</p>",
      "text": "Early dismissal",
      "isHtml": true,
      "isBlank": false
